Accept an AAC configuration whose reserved channel bits an A2DP 1.3 sink left set (Y2Linux).

A sink that configures our AAC source endpoint (AirPods Pro reconnecting by
themselves after the player woke) sends octet 3 = 0x07: stereo (0x04) plus the
two low channel bits, reserved in A2DP 1.3 and read as 5.1 and 7.1 under A2DP
1.4. Intersected with our capabilities (fdk-aac offers 5.1 and 7.1) that is no
single channel mode, so the configuration was refused with
A2DP.InvalidChannels: the link stayed connected without audio until the
platform's radio daemon cancelled it 8 s later and connected the headset itself
(captured with btmon on the developer's player, 2026-11-03; our own
configuration sends 0x84, 48 kHz stereo, and was always accepted). Stereo (or
mono) together with those bits is now read as just stereo (or mono), in the
copy the check and the new transport both use.

--- a/src/bluez.c
+++ b/src/bluez.c
@@ -507,6 +507,23 @@
 			const void *data = g_variant_get_fixed_array(value, &size, sizeof(char));
 			memcpy(&configuration, data, MIN(size, sizeof(configuration)));
 
+			/* Y2: an A2DP 1.3 sink that configures us may leave AAC's two low
+			 * channel bits set; they are reserved in A2DP 1.3 and mean 5.1 and
+			 * 7.1 in 1.4, so stereo plus them is no valid mode and the
+			 * configuration was refused (AirPods Pro reconnecting by
+			 * themselves: octet 3 = 0x07, A2DP.InvalidChannels). Their pick
+			 * is stereo (or mono): keep that one. */
+			if (sep->config.codec_id == A2DP_CODEC_MPEG24 &&
+					size == sizeof(configuration.aac)) {
+				const uint8_t extra = AAC_CHANNEL_MODE_5_1 | AAC_CHANNEL_MODE_7_1;
+				const uint8_t mode = configuration.aac.channel_mode;
+				if (mode & extra && mode & AAC_CHANNEL_MODE_STEREO)
+					configuration.aac.channel_mode = AAC_CHANNEL_MODE_STEREO;
+				else if (mode & extra && mode & AAC_CHANNEL_MODE_MONO)
+					configuration.aac.channel_mode = AAC_CHANNEL_MODE_MONO;
+				data = &configuration;
+			}
+
 			error_code_t err;
 			if ((err = a2dp_check_configuration(sep, data, size)) != ERROR_CODE_OK) {
 				error("Invalid configuration: %s: %s",
